WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.305
A faster way

00:00:01.117 --> 00:00:02.048
to pull digits

00:00:01.859 --> 00:00:02.649
out of pi

00:00:03.174 --> 00:00:04.447
Robert Smith found

00:00:04.259 --> 00:00:05.367
a new algorithm

00:00:05.179 --> 00:00:06.735
to extract hexadecimal

00:00:06.547 --> 00:00:07.537
digits of pi

00:00:07.525 --> 00:00:08.610
without computing the

00:00:08.422 --> 00:00:09.636
digits before them

00:00:10.174 --> 00:00:11.247
It rearranges a

00:00:11.058 --> 00:00:12.190
series going back

00:00:12.002 --> 00:00:13.004
to Euler so

00:00:12.816 --> 00:00:13.817
terms share one

00:00:13.629 --> 00:00:15.126
denominator and four

00:00:14.938 --> 00:00:16.034
remainders can be

00:00:15.846 --> 00:00:16.966
added and divided

00:00:16.778 --> 00:00:17.414
once

00:00:17.942 --> 00:00:19.486
Against Huvent's eight-term

00:00:19.298 --> 00:00:20.477
formula it needs

00:00:20.289 --> 00:00:22.022
about 61 percent fewer

00:00:21.833 --> 00:00:23.696
divisions and 38 percent

00:00:23.508 --> 00:00:24.769
fewer full modular

00:00:24.581 --> 00:00:25.854
exponentiations

00:00:26.382 --> 00:00:27.396
Full story on

00:00:27.207 --> 00:00:28.103
the dashboard
